--- Finding (universal_lambda_machine): a call handed a list and, beside it, a value
--- that can fail (`xs = grow[xs, n]` with `n = int['1']`) copies the list at every
--- call, in both modes, so a loop that grows a list through such calls is quadratic.
--- The same at the top level and inside a function.
---
---   crow run -i:  40000 in 5.2 s CPU
---   crow run:     40000 in about 6.9 s (the build cached)
--- Expected: about 0.02 s, as with `n = 1` (a plain int): 0.02 s interpreted, and
--- the compiled binary as quick. A failure of n would stop the call before grow
--- runs, so the list need not be kept: it can be moved into the call as it is when
--- every argument is plain.
fn grow[xs, n]
    xs = push[xs, n]
    return xs

n = int['1']
xs = []
r = 0
while r lt 40000
    xs = grow[xs, n]
    r = r + 1
log[len[xs]]
